  <h2>What is a 'Batch'? The Factory ecosystem explained</h2>
  <p>
    Unlike traditional fashion houses, replica production is decentralized. Individual private factories buy original retail shoes, dismantle them to create molds, purchase matching raw materials, and run production batches. Some factories specialize in raw speed and budget-friendly volume, while others invest massive capital into premium accuracy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Over time, specific factories gain legendary status in the community for their consistency. These are given acronyms (batches) that spreadsheet curators use to categorize listings. Understanding these shorthand codes is vital as you browse your next haul.
  </p>

  <h2>The Big Three: Legendary Batches and Their Specialties</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>GX Batch:</strong> The undisputed gold standard for Jordan 4s. GX utilizes correct leather thicknesses, precise net alignment, and has the iconic "snappy" back tab. When you purchase a ¥450 GX batch item through a <strong><a href="/">Kakobuy Spreadsheet</a></strong> link, you are paying for original retail molds.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>LJR Batch:</strong> The holy grail for classic Jordan 1s. LJR is legendary for using premium, full-grain leather that creases naturally like retail, rather than the synthetic, plastic-coated polyurethane (PU) leather found in budget ¥150 batches.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>PK 4.0 / PK Batch:</strong> The masters of Nike Dunks, Travis Scott collaborations, and Yeezys. PK excels at dye and color matching, ensuring that subtle shades (like mocha or olive suede) do not look washed out or incorrect under warehouse lighting.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Budget vs. High-Tier: Where Does Your Money Actually Go?</h2>
